  "result": "I opened the issue: https://github.com/acme/textstats/issues/13\n\nI checked the bug before filing it: `word_count(\"\")` really does return 1. The cause is in `textstats/__init__.py:6`, which splits on a literal space with `text.split(\" \")`. Splitting an empty string that way gives `[\"\"]`, a list with one item, so the count is 1.\n\nThe issue also notes two related problems with the same line:\n- **Extra spaces are counted as words.** A string of three spaces returns 4, and `\"a  b\"` returns 3.\n- **Tabs and newlines aren't treated as word breaks.**\n\nIt suggests using `text.split()` with no argument, which is what `average_word_length` already does, and adding a test that an empty string returns 0. I didn't change any code.",
